#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Year 3 Creative Arts Written Core batch 07: creating, combining, presenting and reflecting w37-l1 through w40-l2 (8)."""
from arts_support import L, pv, create, VIS, MUS, DRA, DAN

LESSONS = [
L('s2-arts-y3-w37-l1', VIS, ['a draft', 'a sketch', 'a visual element', 'a decision'],
  "Making your showcase artwork starts with a draft, which is a first version you expect to improve. Make a thumbnail sketch, choose the lines, shapes, colours and textures that carry your theme and decide the materials. Make the artwork in stages: outline first, then big areas, then details. Stop halfway to look at your work from a distance and decide what to change. Artists keep a record of their decisions so they can explain them later. Keep your workspace tidy and your materials safe.",
  "Example: for the theme 'Water', Lena sketches a wave using curved lines. She decides on cool blues and greens with white tips for texture and chooses torn paper collage. She makes the outline first, tears the large waves next and adds foam last. At the halfway point she steps back and sees that the waves look flat, so she adds darker blue layers underneath. Her note says: 'Overlap gave depth.'",
  "Your showcase theme is 'Seasons'. Choose a subject, two visual elements and a first step for a draft artwork. Say how you will check your work halfway.",
  "Strong answer: 'Subject: a tree in autumn. Elements: warm colours (orange, red) and curved lines for branches. First step: a thumbnail sketch. Halfway I will stand back and see if the tree is the focal point.' The plan names elements and a checkpoint.",
  "Thinking that the first idea must be perfect and that changing it means you made a mistake. Artists change their work as they go, and drafts are meant to be improved.",
  "Make a thumbnail sketch and a draft artwork for your showcase. Write three decisions you made with a reason for each. At the halfway point, record one change you made after looking from a distance."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w37-l2', MUS, ['a rehearsal', 'a section', 'a tricky bar', 'refining'],
  "Rehearsing a music piece means repeating it to improve. First play it through, then find the tricky part, such as a fast rhythm or a loud-to-soft change. Practise that part slowly, then at the right tempo, and join it back into the piece. Refining means making small changes to improve the sound, such as adjusting the dynamics, balance or tempo. Record what you changed. Keep the volume safe for your ears, rest your voice or hands, and finish by playing the whole piece.",
  "Example: Kai's group plays 'Rain'. The crescendo in the middle is too sudden. They isolate that part, play it slowly four times with a gradually widening gesture and then speed up. They also lower the drum so the shakers can be heard. They then play the whole piece. Their note: 'Crescendo smoother. Drum softer. Count in properly.'",
  "A group plays well but speeds up in the loud section. Write two steps for fixing the problem and one thing to check afterwards.",
  "Strong answer: 'Play only that section slowly with a metronome or tapped beat, then speed up gradually. Then play the full piece. Afterwards check that the beat stayed steady.' Isolating the tricky part and checking the result is how to refine.",
  "Believing that repeating the whole piece again and again is the best way to rehearse. Focusing on one tricky part, then putting it back, usually works faster.",
  "Rehearse a piece for your showcase. Choose one tricky section, practise it slowly and then at full tempo. Record two changes you made to dynamics, tempo or balance, then play the whole piece for family."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w38-l1', DAN, ['art forms together', 'music and movement', 'a link', 'a transition'],
  "Artists combine art forms to make a stronger piece. A dance can have its own music or be accompanied by live sounds. A drama can include movement and a painted backdrop. The art forms must link: the music fits the dance's mood and the backdrop fits the story. Decide who leads, so the performance does not get messy: for example, the music starts first, then the dancer joins on beat 1. Practise the transitions between parts. Keep the space clear and tidy for the performers and instruments.",
  "Example: for the theme 'Water', a group combines a dance, a rain rhythm and a painted wave backdrop. The shaker group starts softly, the dancers enter on the count of 4 and flow in curved pathways. The music crescendos as the waves grow, and the painted backdrop shows large blue waves. They rehearse the entry points so the music and dance meet at the same moment.",
  "Plan how a poem reading and a short dance could combine for the theme 'Seasons'. Say who starts and how they link.",
  "Strong answer: 'The reader starts with the first season, then the dancer enters on a slow count of 4 and moves to match the words. Light, quick steps for spring and heavy, slow steps for winter.' The art forms support each other with a clear start and link.",
  "Thinking that combining art forms means everything happens at once. A good combination has clear order, with each form supporting the other without hiding it.",
  "Plan a combined piece using two art forms for your showcase theme. Write who starts, how they link and what each art form adds. Rehearse once and note one change that improves the link."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w38-l2', VIS, ['a programme', 'a display', 'a title card', 'a welcome sign'],
  "A showcase needs a programme and a display. A programme lists the items in order with titles and names, and may include a short welcome and a thank-you. A title card names the theme and the artist. Display artworks at eye level with space between them, and arrange them so the audience can follow a path. Use clear lettering, enough contrast between text and background and correct spelling. Set up walkways clear and keep chairs and equipment safe. Ask for help to carry or hang anything heavy.",
  "Example: for the 'Water' showcase, Mia designs a programme: 1. Wave Collage Display. 2. Rain Rhythm (Cleo, Dev). 3. Drought Drama (Ava, Sam). 4. Flowing Dance (Mia, Ben). Thank you for coming. She uses dark blue text on pale paper so it is easy to read, checks the spelling of each name and arranges the collage at eye level with a title card beside each.",
  "Write a three-item programme for a showcase with a welcome and a thank-you. Say one thing you would do so it is easy to read.",
  "Strong answer: 'Welcome. 1. Seasons Poster Display. 2. Song of Four Seasons (class). 3. Autumn Dance (Zoe, Sam). Thank you for coming. I would use dark writing on light paper and check names.' It has an order, a welcome, a thank-you and clear lettering.",
  "Thinking that a display just means putting artworks anywhere on a wall. Arranging works at eye level with space and labels helps the audience see and understand each one.",
  "Design a programme and a display plan for your showcase with all items, names, a welcome and a thank-you. Check spelling with an adult. Draw the room layout showing walkways, seats and where the artworks go."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w39-l1', DRA, ['a run-through', 'an entry', 'an exit', 'a cue sheet'],
  "A showcase rehearsal, called a run-through, practises the whole show in order. Performers need to know their entry (when they come on), their exit (when they leave) and who comes next. A cue sheet lists each item, its start cue and its finish cue, such as 'Item 2 starts when the music fades'. Practise moving props and instruments quietly between items. If something goes wrong, stay calm and keep the show moving. After the run-through, discuss what to fix. Keep safe by keeping walkways clear and walking, not running.",
  "Example: during the run-through, Sam's cue sheet says: 'Item 1: Poem. Start: lights down. End: reader bows. Item 2: Dance. Start: drum begins. End: final shape held for 3 counts.' Item 2's dancers miss their entry because they cannot hear the drum, so they agree to start on a visual signal from the leader instead. They fix it and run it again.",
  "Write a cue sheet for two items. For each, give a start cue and a finish cue, and say what you would do if a cue is missed.",
  "Strong answer: 'Item 1: song. Start: leader nods. End: last note and bow. Item 2: scene. Start: lights change. End: actors freeze. If a cue is missed, the leader gives a hand signal.' Clear start and finish cues, plus a backup, keep the show running.",
  "Believing that a run-through is only for performers. Everyone helping, including those moving props and giving cues, needs to practise so the whole show runs smoothly.",
  "Hold a full run-through of your showcase in the order of your programme. Make a cue sheet, practise entries and exits, and write three problems and how you will fix them. Run the show again with the fixes."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w39-l2', DRA, ['a welcome', 'a performance', 'a bow', 'an audience thank-you'],
  "On the day of the showcase, start with a welcome so the audience knows what they will see. Introduce each item briefly. Perform with clear voices and focused bodies, and keep going if something goes wrong. At the end of each item, hold the final pose, wait for the clapping and bow together. Afterwards, thank the audience and the helpers. Audience members sit quietly, watch carefully and clap kindly. Take slow breaths if you feel nervous, and remember it is fine to be nervous.",
  "Example: Mia welcomes everyone: 'Welcome to our Water showcase. Please enjoy our collages, music, drama and dance.' Each group introduces its piece in one sentence. The drama group's prop falls over, so they pick it up and continue in role. At the end of the show, everyone bows together and Mia says, 'Thank you for coming and thank you to our helpers.'",
  "Write a welcome for a showcase and a one-sentence introduction for one item. Add what you will do if something goes wrong.",
  "Strong answer: 'Welcome to our Seasons showcase. Our first item is a song about autumn. If something goes wrong, I will stay calm, fix it and keep going.' The welcome names the show, the introduction names the item and the plan keeps the show going.",
  "Thinking that if something goes wrong, the whole performance is ruined. Audiences enjoy performers who stay calm and keep going.",
  "Present your showcase for family or friends. Include a welcome, introductions, bows and a thank-you. Afterwards, ask for two stars and a wish and write what you would keep and change."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w40-l1', VIS, ['a reflection', 'a portfolio', 'growth', 'a favourite work'],
  "A reflection is looking back at the year to see what you have learned and how you have grown. A portfolio is a collection of your best work, with labels. Choose pieces from different art forms: a drawing or print, a piece of music, a drama and a dance. For each, write what you made, which elements you used and what you would change. Name a skill you have improved, a challenge you overcame and your goals for next year. Be honest and specific, and use evidence from your work.",
  "Example: Ben's reflection: 'My best artwork is my collage because the overlapping layers made it look deep. I learned to mix colours, keep a steady beat and stay in role. A challenge was singing in tune, which I practised by humming. My goal next year is to try a new instrument.' He names a favourite work, skills, a challenge and a goal, using evidence.",
  "Write a four-sentence reflection naming a favourite work, a skill you improved, a challenge and a goal.",
  "Strong answer: 'My favourite work is my printed pattern. I improved at mixing colours. A challenge was keeping time in group dance, so I counted aloud. My goal is to compose a longer piece.' It has all four parts and is specific.",
  "Thinking that a reflection should only mention what went well. Honest reflections name challenges and how you worked on them, since this shows real learning.",
  "Make a portfolio of four pieces from this course, one from each art form if possible. Write a label for each. Then write a one-page reflection with strengths, challenges and goals for next year."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w40-l2', MUS, ['a celebration', 'a thank-you', 'a class song', 'an achievement'],
  "A celebration marks what a group has achieved. At the end of the creative arts year, the class might sing a song together, share work and thank everyone who helped, including teachers, family and community members who shared cultural knowledge. Choose a song everyone can sing in tune and in time. Practise a count-in, a clear finish and a bow. Celebrate each person's effort and growth, and not only the best performance. Everyone who prefers to can join by tapping the beat or helping behind the scenes.",
  "Example: Year 3 ends with a celebration. The class sings 'Row, Row, Row Your Boat' as a round, with a shaker on the beat. Zoe says thank you to her teacher and to the parent who taught the folk dance. Each student shares one thing they are proud of, such as 'I can keep a steady beat.' Everyone claps for each person.",
  "Plan a short class celebration with one song, two thank-yous and a way to share achievements.",
  "Strong answer: 'We sing a song together with a count-in. We thank our teacher and our families for helping us. Everyone shares one thing they are proud of.' It has a song, thanks and sharing, and includes everyone.",
  "Thinking that a celebration is only for the best performers. A celebration recognises everyone's effort and growth, and everyone has a role.",
  "Plan and hold a celebration at home. Choose a song, practise it with a count-in, write two thank-you notes and share one thing you are proud of from the year. Write a short note about what you enjoyed most."),
]

